Sure, I can help you with that. Below is a TypeScript function that sorts an array of objects by their `id` property and then sums up all the `id` values.

First, let's define the structure of the objects in the array. We'll assume each object has an `id` property which is an integer.

Here's the TypeScript code:

```typescript
interface Item {
    id: number;
    // You can add other properties here if needed
}

function sortAndSumIds(items: Item[]): number {
    // Sort the array by the id property
    items.sort((a, b) => a.id - b.id);

    // Sum up all the ids
    const sum = items.reduce((acc, item) => acc + item.id, 0);

    return sum;
}

// Example usage:
const items: Item[] = [
    { id: 3 },
    { id: 1 },
    { id: 2 },
    { id: 5 },
    { id: 4 }
];

const sum = sortAndSumIds(items);
console.log('Sorted items:', items);
console.log('Sum of ids:', sum);
```

In this code:
- We define an `Item` interface that represents the structure of the objects in the array.
- The `sortAndSumIds` function takes an array of `Item` objects, sorts them by the `id` property, and then calculates the sum of all `id` values.
- The `sort` method sorts the array in place, and the `reduce` method is used to sum up the `id` values.

You can run this code in a TypeScript environment to see the sorted array and the sum of the `id` values.
